Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to write and run JUnit 5 tests in a plain Java project (Maven‑style).  
It consists of:

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `Calculator.java` | A tiny production class with one public method. |
| `CalculatorTest.java` | A JUnit 5 test class exercising the `add()` method. |
| `pom.xml` | (Optional) Maven coordinates that bring JUnit 5 into the classpath. |

---

### 1. Production code – `Calculator.java`

```java
package com.example;

public class Calculator {

    /** Adds two integers and returns the sum. */
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

---

### 2. Test code – `CalculatorTest.java`

```java
package com.example;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.DisplayName;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.ParameterizedTest;
import org.junit.jupiter.params.provider.CsvSource;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class CalculatorTest {

    private Calculator calculator;

    // Executed before each test method
    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        calculator = new Calculator();
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Adding two positive numbers")
    void testAddPositiveNumbers() {
        int result = calculator.add(3, 7);
        assertEquals(10, result, "3 + 7 should equal 10");
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Adding a positive and a negative number")
    void testAddPositiveAndNegative() {
        assertEquals(2, calculator.add(5, -3));
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("Adding two zeros")
    void testAddZeros() {
        assertEquals(0, calculator.add(0, 0));
    }

    /**
     * Parameterised test: runs once per row in the CSV source.
     */
    @ParameterizedTest(name = "{index} => a={0}, b={1}, expected={2}")
    @CsvSource({
        "1, 1, 2",
        "2, 3, 5",
        "-1, -1, -2",
        "0, 5, 5",
        "7, -7, 0"
    })
    @DisplayName("add() with a variety of input pairs")
    void testAddParameterized(int a, int b, int expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, calculator.add(a, b));
    }

    /**
     * Nested test class to group related tests.
     */
    @Nested
    @DisplayName("Edge cases")
    class EdgeCases {

        @Test
        void testAddLargeNumbers() {
            assertEquals(Integer.MAX_VALUE, calculator.add(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 0));
        }

        @Test
        void testAddOverflow() {
            // This will wrap around because of integer overflow
            assertEquals(Integer.MIN_VALUE, calculator.add(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 1));
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 3. (Optional) Maven dependency – `pom.xml`

```xml
<dependencies>
    <!-- JUnit 5 (Jupiter) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        <version>5.10.0</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

> **How to run the tests**
>
> * From an IDE: right‑click `CalculatorTest` → **Run As** → **JUnit Test**.  
> * From the command line (Maven): `mvn test`.  
> * From the command line (Gradle): `./gradlew test`.  

The test class demonstrates a few key JUnit 5 features:

| Feature | Example |
|---------|---------|
| `@Test` | Marks a test method. |
| `@BeforeEach` | Runs before every test to set up common state. |
| `@ParameterizedTest` + `@CsvSource` | Runs the same test logic with many inputs. |
| `@Nested` | Groups related tests under a common context. |
| `assertEquals` | Simple assertion. |
| `@DisplayName` | Gives readable names in test reports. |

Feel free to adapt the code, add more methods to `Calculator`, or extend the tests to suit your real‑world project.
